[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Handling User Input, Touch Responders, and PanResponder API in React Native"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building interactive mobile applications requires a deep understanding of how users interact with their screens. React Native provides robust abstractions for managing touches, gestures, and user input. This tutorial explores standard touch components and dives deep into the PanResponder API for advanced gesture handling."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Basic Touch Responders"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before jumping into complex gestures, React Native offers built-in components like TouchableOpacity, TouchableHighlight, and Pressable. Pressable is the recommended core component for handling touch interactions as it provides fine-grained control over press states."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import { Text, Pressable, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const BasicButton = () => {
  return (
    <Pressable
      onPress={() => console.log('Pressed!')}
      style={({ pressed }) => [
        { backgroundColor: pressed ? 'rgb(210, 230, 255)' : 'white' },
        styles.button
      ]}
    >
      <Text style={styles.text}>Click Me</Text>
    </Pressable>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  button: { padding: 10, borderRadius: 5, borderWidth: 1, borderColor: '#ccc' },
  text: { fontSize: 16, textAlign: 'center' }
});"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Introduction to the PanResponder API"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you need to handle complex gestures like dragging, swiping, or multi-touch movements, standard components are not enough. The PanResponder API reconciles several touches into a single gesture responder. It grants your JavaScript code granular access to the touch lifecycle."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onStartShouldSetPanResponder: Called when a touch starts. Return true to claim the responder."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onMoveShouldSetPanResponder: Called when the user moves their finger. Return true to capture the pan gesture."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onPanResponderGrant: The gesture handler has been granted authorization to respond to touch events."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onPanResponderMove: The most frequently used callback, fired continuously as the user drags their finger."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"onPanResponderRelease: Triggered when the user lifts their finger off the screen."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Building a Draggable Component"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let us combine PanResponder with React Native's Animated API to build a smooth, draggable box that follows the user's finger across the screen."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useRef } from 'react';
import { StyleSheet, View, Animated, PanResponder } from 'react-native';

const DraggableBox = () => {
  const pan = useRef(new Animated.ValueXY()).current;

  const panResponder = useRef(
    PanResponder.create({
      onStartShouldSetPanResponder: () => true,
      onPanResponderGrant: () => {
        pan.setOffset({
          x: pan.x._value,
          y: pan.y._value,
        });
      },
      onPanResponderMove: Animated.event(
        [null, { dx: pan.x, dy: pan.y }],
        { useNativeDriver: false }
      ),
      onPanResponderRelease: () => {
        pan.flattenOffset();
      },
    })
  ).current;

  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Animated.View
        style={[
          styles.box,
          { transform: [{ translateX: pan.x }, { translateY: pan.y }] }
        ]}
        {...panResponder.panHandlers}
      />
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { flex: 1, justifyContent: 'center', alignItems: 'center' },
  box: { width: 100, height: 100, backgroundColor: '#61dafb', borderRadius: 10 }
});

export default DraggableBox;"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Best Practices and Performance Tips"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use useNativeDriver: true where applicable, though note that layout properties like width, height, and direct pan offsets sometimes require JavaScript thread interpolation unless using Reanimated."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always clean up offsets using pan.flattenOffset() on gesture release to prevent compounding offsets during subsequent drags."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider using React Native Reanimated and Gesture Handler libraries for production-ready, performant gesture handling that runs completely on the UI thread."}]
